HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) happens when a request contains duplicate parameters, e.g.:

```http
GET /search?q=safe&q=<script>alert(1)</script> HTTP/1.1
```

A WAF may validate one occurrence while the application uses another, or the app may parse the parameter differently than the WAF.

Example vulnerable Flask app:

```python
from flask import Flask, request

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/search")
def search():
    # Vulnerable: depends on parser behavior for duplicate params
    q = request.args.get("q")
    return f"q={q}"

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run()
```

Safer version: reject duplicates and validate the final parsed value:

```python
from flask import Flask, request, abort

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/search")
def search():
    values = request.args.getlist("q")

    # Reject HPP instead of guessing which value to trust
    if len(values) != 1:
        abort(400, "Duplicate or missing q parameter")

    q = values[0]

    # Validate after parsing, not before
    if not q.isalnum():
        abort(400, "Invalid q")

    return f"q={q}"
```

